tood that it  was  it was somewhat of a symbolic gesture  but it was a really important  demonstration of the  of what people were feeling at the time  what they were feeling was fear of being  caught in the crossfire of a nuclear war  between the u.s and the soviet union and  of course it's cities that were the  target of nuclear weapons and it was  cities around the world that were  leading  a globalized peace movement vancouver's  peace movement was led by a group called  end the arms race which would stage  massive marches for peace  it became so  popular in main street we would have  cops wearing you know badges saying  peace we would have the mayor we would  have you know city councilors like  everybody wanted to be there  in 1989 libby and some other protesters  put the words on the sign into action by  swimming out to the uss independence  which had docked in vancouver  vancouver weapons free zone you have  nuclear bombs on board we ask you to  leave our harbor right now  and every time i came up for a breath i  could see the sailors above me  uh hurling insults um and you know it  was to make a point that that  we didn't mind the the uss independence  being in the poor but we just didn't  want the nuclear weapons concern over  nuclear weapons faded after the fall of  the soviet union and most of these signs  eventually came down  by the city's hand they were never they  were never vandalized they would never i  never remember anything where people  were like you know that stupid or that  silly it was sort of taken seriously  like this is who we are this is what we  want our city to be  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm nitu garcia tonight  your canada is the view from mount  seymour in north vancouver bc thanks for  watching have a great night  you
 

